00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.539 align:start position:0%
African swine fever is an infectious and  deadly animal disease affecting domestic  pigs and wild boar at global level  although not affected humans play a  crucial role in the spread of the virus  being in contact with affected animals  or infected clothes and vehicles can  transmit it to other areas the disease  is mainly transmitted by a direct animal  contact or dissemination of contaminated  pork the disease is not transmittable to  people the EU has been promoting many  initiatives to prevent the further  spread of ASF also at international  level the EU assists Member States in  guaranteeing that only safe unaffected  pork and pigs are treated affected  regions are being monitored and managed  to prevent further spread of the disease  in order to eradicate it  EU veterinary emergency team missions on  the ground support competent veterinary  authorities to prevent control and  eradicate ASF the EU gives priority to a  science-based approach since no vaccine  currently exists the EU is investing in  the development of a candidate vaccine  control measures are also being  developed based on a scientific input  from EPSA an effective surveillance  system has been in place for many years  in all EU Member States EU funds are  allocated and integrated in support  measures for farmers and for in you  trade this coordinated approach is  paying off as the EU was able to limit  and slow down the disease's progress and  even eradicated it in certain areas find  out more about the EU strategic approach  to African swine fever at the following  link  you
 

